log (Hello World!);
log int8:100;

`
There is no reason to declare the
type of an implicit value like above.
Since these values are processed
pre-runtime, any performance difference
would not matter.

Type declarations make more sense
in something like variable declaration,
because this is handled at runtime.

For example, to make an integer with
an 8-byte allowance:
make int:8 number_var;

And later modify it:
set number_var 12;

Since "12" is implicit and will be
parsed before runtime, this will not
make any performance difference during
runtime and a type declaration is not
needed.
`

`Comment`
    `Comment with indent`

make newVar (this is a string);
log ref:newVar;